====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be very tall; to reach a high height.
#: {{ux|en|The office block '''towered''' into the sky.}}
#: {{ux|en|The tree will eventually '''tower''' taller than any of the others.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1921|author=w:Reginald Farrer|title=The Rainbow Bridge|location=London|publisher=E. Arnold & Co.|chapter=10|page=181|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/009011276
|passage={{w|Potentilla}} and Ivory [[w:Daphne (plant)|Daphne]] sat humpily about on the unfolded lawns, and ahead, there '''towered''' out enormous cliffs and fantastic pinnacles of what looked like Dolomite.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1954|month=August|author=H. M. Madgwick|title=The Blaenau Festiniog Tunnel|journal=Railway Magazine|page=569|text=This is itself a cheerless spot, particularly on a rainy day, when, overshadowed by the great massif of rock that '''towers''' in the background, and surrounded by the grey and cheerless quarries, it has a depressing character much in contrast with the green verdure encountered on the northern end of this interesting branch line.}} 
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-08-03|volume=408|issue=8847|magazine=w:The Economist| title=[http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21582512-explosion-start-ups-changing-finance-better-revenge-nerds Revenge of the nerds]|passage=Think of banking today and the image is of grey-suited men in '''towering''' skyscrapers.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be high or lofty; to [[soar]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|II|i|passage=My lord protector's hawks do '''tower''' so well.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1829|author=w:Edgar Allan Poe|chapter={{w|Tamerlane (poem)|Tamerlane}}|title=Al Aaraaf, Tamerlane and Minor Poems|passage=When Hope, the eagle that '''tower’d''', could see<br>No cliff beyond him in the sky,<br>His pinions were bent droopingly —<br>And homeward turn’d his soften’d eye.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1951|month=January|author=H. A. Vallance|title=Kyle of Lochalsh Revisited|journal=Railway Magazine|page=14|text=As we breasted the first summit, the precipitous mass of the Raven's Rock, '''towering''' some 250 ft. above the railway, looked grim and forbidding in the failing light, and distant Ben Wyves was shrouded in mist.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1960|month=December|author=Voyageur|title=The Mountain Railways of the Bernese Oberland|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=752|text=To the left '''towers''' the Jungfrau, with the train heading directly towards it.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|transitive}} To [[soar]] into.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|passage=Her state with oary feet; yet oft they quit <br>The dank, and, rising on stiff pennons, '''tower''' <br>The mid aerial sky|year=1873}}

