===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*dewk-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|touchen}}, {{m|enm|tochen}}, from {{der|en|fro|tochier|t=to touch}} (whence Modern {{cog|fr|toucher}}; compare French doublet {{m|fr|toquer||to offend, bother, harass}}), from {{der|en|la-vul|*tuccō|t=to knock, strike, offend}}, from {{der|en|frk|*tukkōn|t=to knock, strike, touch}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*tukkōną|t=to tug, grab, grasp}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dewk-|t=to draw, pull, lead}}. Largely displaced native {{ncog|enm|rinen}}, from {{ncog|ang|hrīnan}} (whence {{w|Modern English}} {{m|en|rine}}). Doublet of {{m|en|tuck}}.

{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|goh|zochhōn}}, {{m|goh|zuhhōn|t=to grasp, take, seize, snatch}} (whence {{cog|de|zucken||to jerk, flinch}}), {{cog|nds-de|tucken}}, {{m|nds-de|tocken||to fidget, twitch, pull up, entice, throb, knock, repeatedly tap}}, {{cog|dum|tocken}}, {{m|odt|tucken|t=to touch, entice}} (whence {{cog|nl|tokkelen||to strum, pluck}}), {{cog|ang|tucian}}, {{m|ang|tūcian|t=to disturb, mistreat}} (whence Modern {{m+|en|tuck}}). Compare also {{noncog|goh|tokkōn}}, {{m|goh|tockōn|t=to abut, collide}}. More at [[tuck]].

Via {{m+|ine-pro|*dewk-}} cognate with {{m+|en|tie}}, {{m|en|tow}}, {{m|en|tug}}, {{m|en|team}}, {{m+|la|dūcō}}, {{m|la|dux}}.  
