##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Frances A. Harmon|title=The Olympic Games—for Good and All|editor=[[w:John H. Johnson|John H[arold] Johnson]]|journal=[[w:Ebony (magazine)|Ebony Jr!]]|location=Chicago, Ill.|publisher=w:Johnson Publishing Company|month=June–July|year=1984|volume=12|issue=2|page=18|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=5MQDAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA18|column=2|issn=0091-8660|oclc=1787757|passage=Eleven days before the start of the {{quote-gloss|modern Olympic}} Games, a flaming '''torch''' is ignited by the sun in Olympia at the ruins of the ancient Temple of Zeus. With that flame, relay runners lighted '''torches''' one from the other and carry it to the opening of the Games. Ever since 1936, the Summer Olympics has had its fire-font lit by a relay '''torch''' ignited at Olympia.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Rohit Mahajan; Ashish Kumar Sen|title=Ah, the Human Race|editor=w:Vinod Mehta|journal=[[w:Outlook (Indian magazine)|Outlook: The Weekly Newsmagazine]]|location=New Delhi|publisher=Maheshwer Peri on behalf of Outlook Publishing|start_date=22 April 2008|date=28 April 2008|page=48|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=OTEEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA60|oclc=909709349|passage=The degradation of the {{quote-gloss|Olympic}} '''torch''' worldwide—it had to be snuffed out more than once to protect it from protesters—even provoked angry Chinese students to mobilise "150 strong and energetic runners" to defend it in Australia, raising the spectre of violence.}}
##* {{quote-web|en|title=Paris 2024 Paralympic Torch: A Symbol of Equality, Water, and Peacefulness|work=Paralympic.org|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240818121839/https://www.paralympic.org/paris-2024/torch|location=Bonn, North Rhine-Westphalia|publisher=w:International Paralympic Committee|accessdate=18 August 2024|passage=The Paris 2024 Paralympic '''Torch''', characterised by its sparkling champaign{{sic|champagne}} colour and unique design, represents the spirit of the Games. {{...}} With its gentle curves, the '''torch''' represents a sense of peace and solidarity. It stands as a symbol of the Paralympic Games' mission to promote harmony and unity.}}
