#* {{quote-book|en|author=Leonard Mikeska, witness|chapter=Testimony of Leonard Mikeska, Chief Arson Investigator, Houston Fire Department, Houston, Tex.|title=Arson-for-hire: Hearings before the [[w:United States Senate Homeland Security Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations|Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations]] of the [[w:United States Senate Committee on Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs|Committee on Governmental Affairs]], {{w|United States Senate}}, Ninety-fifth [[w:United States Congress|Congress]], Second Session{{nb...|August 23 and 24; September 13 and 14, 1978: Printed for the Use of the Committee on Governmental Affairs}}|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|U.S. Government Printing Office]]|date=13 September 1978|page=172|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_hEzOxNCOJgC&pg=RA1-PA172|oclc=311634234|passage=Some years ago, another Houston '''torch''' attached a kitchen match to the bell striker on an ordinary telephone so that it would vibrate when the phone rang. He placed a piece of sandpaper close enough to the match so that when the bell rang, the match rubbed the surface of the sandpaper and started a fire. The '''torch''', after setting up the device, drove to a town many miles from his home and then dialed his home number, successfully starting a fire in his own home.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Herbert F. Weisberg; Judith D. Feins; Jan Schreiber|chapter=The Investigative Process: Getting Cases to Prosecution|title=Arson Investigation and Prosecution: A Study of Four Major American Cities|series=National Institute of Justice Research Report|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher={{w|National Institute of Justice}}, [[w:United States Department of Justice|U.S. Department of Justice]]|month=March|year=1984|page=137|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=4Gkn2y93xh8C&pg=PA137|oclc=11061433|passage=Upon the advice of the prosecutor, who was already at the arson unit's office, the '''torches''' were not arrested, but "detained" and offered the opportunity to cooperate with the investigators in their efforts to implicate the landlord. {{...}} As one '''torch''' had had most contact with their employer, investigators decided that he should make a call to the landlord on a tapped telephone and try and discuss the planned fire with him. The landlord was reluctant to say anything over the phone and suggested that he and the '''torch''' should meet.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=David R. Redsicker; John J. O’Connor|chapter=Surveillance|title=Practical Fire and Arson Investigation|edition=2nd|location=Boca Raton, Fla.|publisher=w:CRC Press|year=1997|page=358|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/practicalfirears0000reds/page/358/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-8493-8155-3|passage=A registered confidential informant may provide information, either that a particular building has been targeted to burn or that an identified "'''torch'''" has been contracted to burn some unknown building. In the first instance, fixed surveillance at the building should be started immediately to try to catch the '''torch''' before the act.}}

