#: {{ux|en|I ate a piece of '''toast''' for breakfast.}}
#: {{ux|en|This '''toast''' and marmalade tastes heavenly.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1991|author=w:Stephen Fry|title=The Liar|page=23|passage=Tea was a very special institution, revolving as it did around the ceremony and worship of '''Toast'''. In [public schools] where alcohol, tobacco and drugs were forbidden, it was essential that something should take their place as a powerful and public totem of virility and cool. '''Toast''', for reasons lost in time, was the substance chosen.}}
# {{senseid|en|Q154168}} {{lb|en|countable}} A proposed [[salutation]] (e.g. saying "[[cheers]]") while drinking [[alcohol]].
#: {{syn|en|pledge}}
#: {{ux|en|At the reception, there were many '''toasts''' from the well-wishers.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1960 February|author=R. K. Kirkland|title=The Great Western and Brentford Railway|journal=Railway Magazine|page=79|text=The evening meal was "a repast of most ''recherché'' description," involving an endless series of '''toasts''' to almost everybody (with the notable exception of the other railway already serving Brentford, which came in for some hard knocks from various speakers).}}
# {{senseid|en|to which a salutation is made}} {{lb|en|countable}} A person, group, or [[notable]] object to which a salutation with alcohol is made; a person or group held in similar [[esteem]].
#: {{ux|en|He was the '''toast''' of high society.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:John McWhorter|title=Saint Maya|work=The New Republic|date=2014-05-28|url=https://newrepublic.com/article/117924/saint-maya-angelou-product-blissfully-bygone-america|issn=0028-6583|passage=Josephine Baker did not become the '''toast''' of Paris by just shaking her booty for some theater gypsies as a party wound down.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260217195152/https://newrepublic.com/article/117924/saint-maya-angelou-product-blissfully-bygone-america|archivedate=17 February 2026}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|slang|chiefly|_|US}} {{rfq-sense|en}} Something that is [[irreparably]] damaged or used up, especially when destroyed by heat or fire; something which has been [[burnt to a crisp]] or [[incinerated]].
#: {{ux|en|The metal frame survived the fire, but the plastic and rubber bits are '''toast'''.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|slang|chiefly|_|US}} Something that will be no more; something subject to [[impending]] [[destruction]], [[harm]] or [[injury]].
#: {{syn|en|dead duck|dead meat}}
#: {{coi|en|to become/be '''toast'''}}
#: {{ux|en|If I ever get my hands on the guy that stole my wallet, he’s '''toast'''!}}
#: {{ux|en|You are going to be '''toast''' if you don't submit your school project on time.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|title=w:Ghostbusters|actor=w:Bill Murray|role=w:Peter Venkman|year=1984|text=This chick is '''toast'''!|footer={{small|The original line was: "I'm gonna turn this guy into toast."<ref>{{cite-journal|en|author=Ben Zimmer|authorlink=Ben Zimmer|title='Toast': From Busting Ghosts to Burning Careers|work=The Wall Street Journal|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/toast-from-busting-ghosts-to-burning-careers-ff44b34d|archiveurl=https://archive.ph/0oD3R|archivedate=24 June 2023|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Dow Jones & Company}}|date=22 June 2023|issn=1042-9840|oclc=781541372|passage=In the script, as the Ghostbusters train their proton blasters on Gozer, Murray's character says, "That's it! I'm gonna turn this guy into toast." By the time the scene was shot, the filmmakers had decided the human form of Gozer should be played by the model Slavitza Jovan. Murray improvised various comments about her, including changing the line in the script to "This chick is toast." The Oxford English Dictionary recognizes Murray's ad-lib as the earliest known occurrence of "toast" with the modern meaning, calling the usage "proleptic."}}</ref>}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-GB|author=Joseph Gedeon|quotee=w:Pete Hegseth|title=Pete Hegseth says Iranian regime is ‘toast’ as US expands military offensive|work=The Guardian|date=2026-03-04|url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/mar/04/pete-hegseth-iran-us-israel-war|issn=0261-3077|passage=He {{quote-gloss|P. Hegseth}} said the Iranian regime “are '''toast''', and they know it, or at least soon enough, they will know it.”}}
