===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|set theory}} Any [[cardinal number|cardinal]] or [[ordinal number]] which is larger than any [[finite]], i.e. [[natural number]]; ''often represented by the [[Hebrew]] [[letter]] [[aleph]] ([[ℵ]]) with a subscript 0, 1, etc.''
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1961|author=Jane Muir|title=Of Men and Numbers: The Story of the Great Mathematicians|publisher=Courier Dover Publications|isbn=9780486289731|page=228
|passage=It will be recalled that Cantor called the first '''transfinite number''' &alefsym;<sub>0</sub>. He called the second '''transfinite number'''—the one describing the set of all real numbers— C. It has not been proved whether C is the next '''transfinite number''' after &alefsym;<sub>0</sub> or whether another number exists between them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1968|author=B. T. Levšenko|chapter=Spaces of transfinite dimensionality|title=Fourteen Papers on Algebra, Topology, Algebraic and Differential Geometry|page=141|publisher=American Mathematical Soc.|isbn=9780821817735
|passage=Let <math>R</math> be a bicompact of dimensionality <math>\operatorname{ind}(R) \le \alpha</math>. If <math>\alpha</math> is an isolated '''transfinite number''', than {{sic}} at any point <math>x \in R</math> there exist arbitrarily small neighborhoods <math>Vx</math> with boundaries of dimensionality <math>\operatorname{ind} \overline{Vx} \le \alpha - 1</math>.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1990|author=Joseph Warren Dauben|title=Georg Cantor: His Mathematics and Philosophy of the Infinite|publisher=Princeton University Press|isbn=9780691024479|page=180
|passage=After all, it was the ordinals that made precise definition of the transfinite cardinals possible. And until Cantor had introduced the order types of '''transfinite number''' classes, he could not define precisely any transfinite cardinal beyond the first power.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=John Tabak|title=Numbers: Computers, Philosophers, and the Search for Meaning|publisher=Infobase Publishing|isbn=9780816068746|page=153
|passage=For example, does there exist a '''transfinite number''' that is strictly bigger than &alefsym;<sub>0</sub> and strictly smaller than &alefsym;<sub>1</sub>? In this case an instance of this in between number is too big to be put into one-to-one correspondence with the set of natural numbers, and too small to be put into one-to-one correspondence with the set of real numbers.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Benjamin Wardhaugh|title=A Wealth of Numbers: An Anthology of 500 Years of Popular Mathematics Writing|publisher=Princeton University Press|isbn=9781400841981|page=136
|passage=Having demonstrated the existence of a one-to-one correspondence, we can conclude that the class of the squares of all the natural numbers has the same '''transfinite number''' as the class of all the natural numbers! This result is not what might have been anticipated, seeing that the second class is a proper subset of the first.}}
